/* Three Shores: scenario data for the Simulation tab.
   Source: three-shores-scenario.json (course team storyboard, "Draft for review").
   To update: replace everything after "window.SCENARIO = " with the new JSON
   (see README.md, "Simulation"). Do not edit scoring rules in js/simulation.js. */
window.SCENARIO = {"meta":{"title":"Three Shores","script":"Three Shores","subtitle":"Full branch storyboard","course":"E-learning course on the Labour Dimensions of Climate Change and Human Mobility","intro":"Every decision, every option and every consequence in the climate mobility decision simulation, laid out so that the whole thing can be reviewed without playing it. Generated directly from the scenario data file, so this document cannot drift from what the learner will see.","minutes":30},"indicators":[{"k":"choice","label":"Choice","long":"Whether people can move, stay or return on terms they set themselves. Covers both moving with dignity and staying in place with dignity.","start":50,"pressure":-3},{"k":"voice","label":"Voice","long":"Whether communities, workers' and employers' organisations and the country itself take part in decisions that affect them, and have the power to say no.","start":46,"pressure":-5},{"k":"work","label":"Work","long":"Decent work, livelihoods and social protection that people actually receive, at home, on arrival and after relocation.","start":48,"pressure":-4},{"k":"capacity","label":"Capacity","long":"The fiscal and institutional headroom of the state and its partners to deliver, and to absorb the next shock.","start":54,"pressure":-2}],"places":[["Republic of Alvara","A fictional island state of about 18,000 people. The capital, Marau, is on the main island of Alvara. A composite of the three dossiers, not a portrait of any one country."],["Keru","A chain of low atolls, less than three metres above the sea, home to about 4,500 people. Most do not want to leave. This is the Pacific dossier: Tuvalu and the Falepili Union Treaty."],["Tamal","A small northern island of about 1,500 people with a lagoon, mangroves and land held in common by the community. Struck by Cyclone Edra. This is the Caribbean dossier: the repeal of the Barbuda Land Act."],["Vale","An upland district on the main island, forty minutes from Marau, where the government built 400 houses for relocated families. This is the Southern Africa dossier: social protection and climate resilience in Zimbabwe."],["Halden","A large, wealthy neighbour with labour shortages in care, construction and farming. It has offered Alvara a mobility and finance compact."],["Meridian","A foreign investment group that wants to build a resort, marina and airstrip on Tamal's lagoon shore."],["Workers' Congress and Employers' Federation","Alvara's national workers' and employers' organisations. Together with the government they form the tripartite constituency that the ILO brings to the table."]],"bands":{"choice":["Leaving became the only real option. The pathway existed on paper, but families from Keru and Tamal moved because staying had been made impossible, not because they chose to. Nobody in Alvara uses the phrase moving with dignity without irony.","Some families chose and others were chosen for. The pathway, the fund and the land rules gave a few people a real choice and left many with a thinner version of one.","People in Alvara can go, stay or come back, and they can say which. Keru's elders describe the pathway as an option they hope never to use. That is what moving with dignity and staying with dignity look like when they exist side by side."],"voice":["Decisions arrived from above. Keru heard about the compact on the radio, Tamal heard about the land law from a lawyer and Vale heard about its transfers from a notice board. People stopped expecting to be asked, and stopped answering when they were.","Some rooms listened and others did not. Social partners had a seat in some decisions and a briefing in others, and communities were consulted when it was cheap to do so.","People who live with the decisions took part in making them. The island councils, the Workers' Congress and the Employers' Federation can each point to a clause they argued for, and the compact belongs to them as much as to the government."],"work":["Relocation moved people from one precarious livelihood to another. Day labour at the junction, domestic work behind closed doors, and a support scheme that ended in a cliff. The jobs that existed were not the jobs people were promised.","A floor exists and it is patchy. Some relocated workers have contracts, coverage and a way off support. Many have a transfer and a day of work, when they are lucky.","Relocated and host workers share a floor of contracts, coverage and recognised skills that travels with them. Support was designed to end, and most households left it on their own terms."],"capacity":["The state has spent everything it had, in money, time and goodwill. The next cyclone will find the Treasury, the ministries and the partners where they were: waiting for someone else.","Alvara can manage a normal year and would not survive a bad one. Money comes from a mixture of sources and none of them is secure.","Alvara can carry the next shock. It has funding it controls, institutions that talk to one another and partners who answer the phone."]},"epilogue":{"ballot_as_drafted":"The ballot still draws the winners its rules favour: people with the fee, a good connection and the language. Outer island households and anyone without a reliable signal enter it less often.","labour_annex":"Halden's settlement office handles about forty cases a year. The recognition schedule for care and trades is the part of the compact that workers mention when they are asked what it did for them.","tied_permits":"The seasonal scheme sends money home and sends complaints to the union. Workers cannot change employer without losing the right to stay, and most report nothing until the season ends.","stay_package":"The ring fenced fund has paid for sea walls, rain tanks and two livelihood programmes on Keru. Elders cite it as the reason the pathway feels like an option and not an exit.","security_clause_open":"Article 4 has never been formally invoked. It has shaped every telecommunications and ports decision since. Alvara's ministers say they consult Halden because that is easier than finding out whether they have to.","clause_narrowed":"The review clause has been used twice. Neither review changed the text much, and both changed the way Halden's officials speak to Alvara's.","unbundled":"The pathway has operated for years. The security chapter is still being negotiated and the finance chapter still waits on it. Halden has learned that Alvara will walk away from a package.","regional_route":"The regional template was adopted by three other islands and never used in Alvara. A declaration exists. A pathway does not, and Keru's families are leaving by other routes.","cabinet_signed":"Keru's elders learned the terms of the compact from a relative in Marau. Every consultation in Alvara since has been judged against that first one.","full_consult":"The consultation forum created for the compact still meets. Tamal and Vale both used it, and it is where the Workers' Congress and the Federation first met the island councils.","referendum_held":"The referendum is the only decision about the compact that nobody in Alvara disputes. It cost six months and some of Halden's goodwill, and it gave later governments something to point to.","tripartite_only":"Employers and unions knew what the compact promised. Keru and Tamal did not, and that gap was the first item on the first agenda of the Tripartite Council.","waited":"The airstrip was finished before the first families were allowed home. A third of Tamal's evacuees never went back.","own_damage_data":"The survey is now the baseline for Tamal's reconstruction plan. It is quoted by the Ministry that disputed it.","litigated":"The standing ruling is cited whenever Tamal's residents consider going to court. Few do.","phased_return":"Returning families were first on the repair payroll. The clinic roof was finished in the fourth month, and the airstrip took its first private jet in the second year.","deeds_safeguards":"The community's right of first refusal has been used thirty one times. The Council bought back eight plots, and families bought most of the rest.","consent_rule":"The Tamal consent rule survives in the Compact. It has been tested once, by a developer, and held.","deeds_sold":"A third of Tamal's lagoon frontage belongs to people who do not live there. The cash is spent, and the children ask what was sold.","intl_appeal":"Alvara became a known case. It got a loss and damage mission, a human rights visit and a long article in a foreign newspaper. It did not get the airstrip taken down.","jobs_no_agreement":"The scaffold failure on Tamal was the first death on a building site in Alvara in a decade. The subcontractor closed within the month and reopened under another name.","local_benefit_agreement":"The Tamal agreement is the template that the Federation and the Workers' Congress now use for every large project. It was the first tripartite agreement on the island.","community_enterprise":"The mangrove crews and the fishers' cooperative outlasted their first donor. Their model has been copied at Vale.","moratorium":"The land use plan took eight months. Tamal now has the only adopted plan in the country, and fewer jobs than it was promised.","informal_chapter":"Vale's informal chapter is now the largest single branch of the union. Most of its members are women, and domestic workers lead it.","land_first":"Half a hectare per household is now the settlement standard. Visitors from other countries ask who negotiated it, and the answer is a union.","coop_started":"The savings group is the oldest institution at Vale that neither the state nor the union runs. It is where people hear about jobs first.","insiders_only":"Union density at Vale is two per cent. The union has a good safety agreement and no mandate to speak for the junction.","targeted_cash":"The transfers ended in the twenty fourth month. Households with land or a job moved on, and the rest were back in the queue.","green_works_ega":"The terraces and the school road are still there. So are 600 workers with safety training and a record of days worked.","inclusive_coverage":"Resentment between the lower village and Vale is lower than anywhere else in the country. The scheme costs more than the Treasury planned and still appears in its budget.","grievance_mech":"The grievance desk handles about forty cases a month in languages workers use, through a route that does not run back through the person who recruited them. That last feature is why it works.","inspection_complaint":"The inspectorate's closure rate is good. Workers in private households still do not appear in its statistics.","employer_deal":"The sector agreement covers the Federation's members. The two firms that paid workers sixty per cent of the floor are not among them, and the agreement says nothing about them.","action_taken":"The stoppage is remembered as the day Vale was on the evening news, and as the day eleven people lost their jobs.","hiring_hall":"The hiring hall places workers without fees and without brokers. A foreign delegation asked to copy it, and the union had to explain that it was not a business.","hiring_pledge":"One in four new vacancies in member firms now goes to relocated workers. The standard contract written for the pledge has become the template for every large employer.","skills_partnership":"The recognition framework works at both ends. Nurses who leave for Halden can return and be recognised, and Halden's employers know what an Alvaran certificate means.","flex_contracts":"The two tier wage was abolished after eighteen months. Turnover among trainees was the highest in the country, and the Federation lost its seat on the working group.","employers_absent":"The hiring rules were written without the Federation. Members say they are unworkable, and the Federation is asked why it did not say so earlier.","payroll_share":"The contribution funds portable coverage for people who move, return or relocate. It is the one part of the Compact that does not depend on a donor.","halden_funds":"Halden's fund covers the first three years. After that Alvara will need to find the money, or accept what comes with it.","investor_levy":"The levy raised less than the Treasury hoped. It paid for the monitoring body, which nobody had budgeted for.","donor_dependent":"The first tranche arrived after eighteen months. The Compact is now written around the donor's calendar, which is not Alvara's.","compact_signed":"The Compact was adopted by acclamation. Its monitoring body meets every quarter and publishes what it finds.","compact_reservations":"The Compact was adopted with a reservation recorded in the minutes. The reservation on the land consent rule has been quoted by every investor since.","signature_withheld":"The Compact was adopted after a two month delay. The financing was settled on terms the Federation did not set.","review_invoked":"Halden's labour and foreign ministries sat at Alvara's tripartite table. The list in Article 4 is now published, narrowed and reviewed every three years."},"debrief":["Which decision across the four acts gave away the most that could not be recovered? Who had the authority to take a different decision at the time, and what would they have needed in order to do it?","In act one a mobility pathway could be bought with part of the country's sovereignty. In act two investment could be bought with land. What do these trades have in common, and what would you treat as non negotiable?","Which of your choices protected the people in front of you while weakening the system behind them, or the reverse? What would you need in order to avoid that trade off?","Employers and workers' organisations were absent, late or partial in most acts. Where did their presence change the outcome, and what would it have taken to have them in the room earlier?","Which of the three dossiers (Pacific, Caribbean, Southern Africa) transferred best into Alvara, and which transferred worst? What would you adapt before offering it to a peer through South South and Triangular Cooperation?","What in this simulation would look different in the country, sector or corridor where you actually work, and what would look exactly the same?"],"instruments":["ILO Guidelines for a Just Transition towards Environmentally Sustainable Economies and Societies for All (2015), and the 2023 International Labour Conference conclusions on just transition","ILO Employment and Decent Work for Peace and Resilience Recommendation, 2017 (No. 205)","ILO Social Protection Floors Recommendation, 2012 (No. 202)","ILO Transition from the Informal to the Formal Economy Recommendation, 2015 (No. 204)","ILO Declaration on Fundamental Principles and Rights at Work (1998, amended 2022)","Migration for Employment Convention (Revised), 1949 (No. 97) and Migrant Workers (Supplementary Provisions) Convention, 1975 (No. 143)","ILO Human Resources Development Recommendation, 2004 (No. 195)","ILO General Principles and Operational Guidelines for Fair Recruitment, and the definition of recruitment fees and related costs","ILO Tripartite Declaration of Principles concerning Multinational Enterprises and Social Policy","ILO and FAO Livelihood Assessment Toolkit; ILO cash for work experience in Fiji and Solomon Islands","Paris Agreement (2015), Warsaw International Mechanism and the Loss and Damage Fund established at COP27","Global Compact for Safe, Orderly and Regular Migration (2018), Objectives 5, 6 and 21","Guiding Principles on Internal Displacement (1998) and the Nansen Initiative Protection Agenda (2015)","Pacific Regional Framework on Climate Mobility (2023) and the Pacific Climate Change, Migration and Human Security Programme","Fiji's Planned Relocation Guidelines","UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights, including the effectiveness criteria for operational level grievance mechanisms"],"scoring":{"loss":"round(net*0.5)","gain":"round(net*0.5*(100-level)/50)","net":"raw + variant shifts + standing pressure","clamp":[0,100],"bands":[[0,34],[35,64],[65,100]]},"acts":[{"num":1,"role":"The negotiator","who":"Senior labour adviser to the Prime Minister's negotiating team","place":"Ministry of Labour, Marau, Republic of Alvara","year":"Year 0. Six weeks before signing.","brief":"The Keru atolls sit less than three metres above the sea, and about 4,500 people live on them. Halden has put a draft compact on the table: a permanent residence pathway for Alvarans, a ballot for 280 places a year, and a fund for coastal works. You are the only person on the negotiating team whose job is labour. The Prime Minister wants to sign before the cyclone season, and the opposition has not seen the text.","lens":"Every option here can be defended in Parliament. The question is what the person who actually moves will have when they arrive, what the person who stays will be offered, and what Alvara gives away to get both.","bridge":"Whatever was signed, or not signed, it now governs the lives of the 4,500 people on Keru. The sea does not wait for the next round of talks. A year later, a different kind of emergency arrives on a different island. Step into the chair of someone who has no seat at the negotiating table at all.","dossier":{"case":"Tuvalu and the Falepili Union Treaty Agreement","tag":"Pacific","points":["Signed on 9 November 2023. Creates a special mobility pathway: up to 280 visas a year, allocated by ballot, with permanent residence, a route to citizenship and the freedom to take work of one's choice.","Article 3 frames the pathway as mobility with dignity. Article 4 requires Tuvalu to agree with Australia any security and defence related arrangement with another state, in terms that are 'not limited to' a list.","Critics argued the text was not consulted widely enough and should have gone to referendum. The case brief asks whether the treaty is still climate mobility justice once its trade offs are read.","Most Tuvaluans do not want to leave. Many describe the benefits of moving as being for their children, and fear the stigma of the label 'climate migrant'."],"gq":"Tuvalu guiding questions 1 to 5 (SSTC between Pacific and Caribbean SIDS, just policies, 'migration with dignity', the roles of ILO constituents)."},"scenes":[{"id":"1.1","title":"The pathway","situation":"Halden's draft lets Alvarans aged 18 and over enter a ballot for 280 permanent residence places a year. Spouses and children can be included. Applicants must meet character and health requirements. After that the text says very little about work: Halden's negotiator says everything else is a matter for Halden's ordinary law. Alvara's care workers, builders and fishers are already leaving in small numbers by other routes, and the Minister wants to know what, if anything, the labour side should ask for.","options":[{"id":"1.1a","title":"Accept the pathway as drafted and spend your effort elsewhere","note":"Fast. The ballot is simple.","raw":{"choice":8,"voice":0,"work":-12,"capacity":12},"sets":["ballot_as_drafted"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The text is agreed in a day. Residence is permanent and holders can take any job. The first ballot is won mostly by people with money for the fee, a reliable internet connection and good English. Within a year, the first arrivals report that Halden does not recognise their nursing and trade certificates, and many are working below their skills.","instrument":"Convention No. 143 says that rules for recognising occupational qualifications should be adopted after consulting social partners. The course lists recognition of skills among the questions that any bilateral agreement should answer (Module 4).","practice":"Permanent residence is a strong legal base: people are not tied to an employer. It does not fix skills waste, and skills waste is where the labour market quietly takes back what the visa gave.","echoes":"Tuvalu","reference":"Module 2, Theme 2; Module 4, Theme 1","variants":[]},{"id":"1.1b","title":"Ask for a labour annex: recognition of qualifications, a settlement service and a named focal point for workers","note":"Slower. Needs Halden's labour ministry.","raw":{"choice":10,"voice":4,"work":22,"capacity":-8},"sets":["labour_annex"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"Halden's immigration officials say the annex is not their department. It takes six weeks and a visit by your Minister to Halden's labour ministry. The result is a short schedule: recognition of care and trade qualifications, a settlement office for new arrivals and a contact point for complaints. It is less than you asked for and more than the draft held.","instrument":"The ILO asks countries of origin and destination to settle questions on skills recognition, prior learning and pre departure orientation before a bilateral agreement is signed. The Just Transition Guidelines say international labour standards should be the basis for such agreements (Module 4).","practice":"The Falepili text gave residence and the freedom to work, and said little else on labour. An annex costs time and goodwill, and it is the part of the agreement that workers will touch every day.","echoes":"Tuvalu","reference":"Module 4, Theme 1 and Box 8","variants":[]},{"id":"1.1c","title":"Ask for a larger, faster circular scheme on top: seasonal and temporary work permits","note":"More places. Less permanence.","raw":{"choice":-6,"voice":-4,"work":-20,"capacity":20},"sets":["tied_permits"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"Halden agrees quickly. A temporary scheme for 1,200 seasonal workers a year in farming and construction is added to the ballot. Remittances start within months and the Treasury is relieved. The permits are tied to one employer, and a worker who leaves a bad employer loses the right to stay.","instrument":"Temporary labour migration schemes may deny workers the freedom to change employer and equal pay for equal work (ILO 2022, cited in Module 2). The fundamental principles and rights at work apply to every worker, without exception.","practice":"Remittances are a real adaptation resource and so is the exposure that comes with them. Employer tied permits are the standard mechanism by which a good job becomes a trap.","echoes":"Handbook","reference":"Module 2, Theme 2","variants":[]},{"id":"1.1d","title":"Make the pathway one half of a package: ring fence a share of the adaptation fund for people who stay","note":"Protects the right to remain.","raw":{"choice":24,"voice":6,"work":5,"capacity":-10},"sets":["stay_package"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"Halden's negotiators are surprised, then interested. Their own ministers like a text that does not read as an exit door. A quarter of the coastal works fund is set aside for livelihoods and services on Keru and for any household that chooses to relocate within Alvara. The fund is smaller and slower to disburse than the headline figure.","instrument":"Staying in place with dignity means land rights protection, resilient infrastructure, access to climate finance and community led planning. The Pacific Regional Framework on Climate Mobility (2023) covers all types of movement, including staying in place (Module 2; Module 4).","practice":"Kiribati moved from promoting migration with dignity towards staying with dignity as local voices pushed back. The Tuvalu brief notes that most people do not want to move, and that movement is often described as being for children and grandchildren.","echoes":"Tuvalu","reference":"Module 2, Theme 1 and Box 1","variants":[]}]},{"id":"1.2","title":"The security clause","situation":"Article 4 of the draft says Alvara will agree with Halden any arrangement it makes with any other state or entity on security and defence related matters. A list follows, including ports, telecommunications and energy infrastructure, and ends with the words 'including but not limited to'. Halden has tied its offer of coastal works finance to the whole text. The Prime Minister's chief of staff wants to sign before the cyclone season.","options":[{"id":"1.2a","title":"Sign Article 4 as drafted","note":"Everything goes through.","raw":{"choice":6,"voice":-30,"work":4,"capacity":20},"sets":["security_clause_open"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The compact is signed within the month. Finance for coastal works is released in the first quarter. The opposition reads Article 4 in the newspaper and calls it a surrender of foreign policy. A constitutional challenge is filed and quietly withdrawn.","instrument":"The principle of common but differentiated responsibilities means that wealthier states carry a heavier burden of climate finance. Finance owed on that basis is not meant to be a purchase of foreign policy (Module 2, restitution and restorative justice).","practice":"The Falepili case brief reads the security chapter as turning a climate assistance blueprint into a tit for tat transaction. The words 'not limited to' leave room for a wide interpretation.","echoes":"Tuvalu","reference":"Module 2, Theme 1","variants":[]},{"id":"1.2b","title":"Narrow it: name the matters it covers, add a duty to consult and a review after five years","note":"Costs five months.","raw":{"choice":4,"voice":16,"work":0,"capacity":-10},"sets":["clause_narrowed"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"Halden's lawyers resist for three months and then concede the review clause, in return for a longer notice period. The text is narrower and still uncomfortable. Finance is delayed until the first review. The Prime Minister is irritated and the opposition says it did not go far enough.","instrument":"The Paris Agreement says capacity building should be country driven and foster country ownership (Article 11). The Just Transition Guidelines make social dialogue an integral part of the institutional framework for policy (Module 3; Module 5).","practice":"A review clause is not glamorous. It is the only part of the text that lets a later government reopen what an earlier one signed, and you will be glad of it in act four.","echoes":"Tuvalu","reference":"Module 3, Theme 1; Module 2, Conclusions","variants":[]},{"id":"1.2c","title":"Unbundle: sign the pathway now and negotiate the security chapter separately","note":"Risks both.","raw":{"choice":12,"voice":8,"work":4,"capacity":-5},"sets":["unbundled"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"Halden agrees to sign the mobility chapter on its own, and says the finance chapter will follow the security discussions. The pathway is in place within six weeks. The security talks are slow and the finance is smaller and later than the headline.","instrument":"Objective 5 of the Global Compact for Migration commits states to enhance the availability and flexibility of regular pathways, including for people compelled to leave by disasters and environmental degradation (Module 2).","practice":"A pathway that stands on its own is easier to defend as a right than as a payment. Halden has learned that Alvara will separate things, and will remember it in the next round.","echoes":"Tuvalu","reference":"Module 2, Theme 2","variants":[]},{"id":"1.2d","title":"Refuse the clause and take the question to regional partners for a template","note":"Slow and unfunded.","raw":{"choice":-12,"voice":20,"work":-4,"capacity":-17},"sets":["regional_route"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"Your peers share three drafts from their own negotiations and one of them offers technical help. Halden's offer is not withdrawn but it lapses at the end of the quarter. The coastal works finance never arrives and the pathway stays at the level of a declaration.","instrument":"South South and Triangular Cooperation lets small island states build context specific frameworks and shift agency back to climate affected nations. The ILO promoted SIDS to SIDS cooperation from 2018 to 2023 (Module 3, Box 2).","practice":"Regional frameworks, such as the 2023 Pacific framework, matter for coherence and solidarity. They are not a fund. Principle has a price when the sea is rising.","echoes":"Handbook","reference":"Module 3, Theme 2 and Box 2","variants":[]}]},{"id":"1.3","title":"Who is in the room","situation":"The draft has leaked. Keru's elders say nobody asked them. The Employers' Federation and the Workers' Congress learned of it from a newspaper, and the opposition demands a referendum. The Prime Minister says there is no time. You are asked to advise on how the text should be taken forward.","options":[{"id":"1.3a","title":"Cabinet signs. Brief the public afterwards","note":"Fastest route to signature.","raw":{"choice":-4,"voice":-24,"work":-4,"capacity":16},"sets":["cabinet_signed"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The compact is signed in the next cabinet meeting. A press conference follows, with a map. Keru's elders hear the details from a relative in Marau. The Workers' Congress files a formal protest, and the Employers' Federation asks, politely, who will pay for the skills gap.","instrument":"The Just Transition Guidelines state that adequate, informed and ongoing consultation should take place with all relevant stakeholders, and that strong social consensus is fundamental (Module 2, Conclusions).","practice":"The Tuvalu brief reports that citizens and members of the opposition said they had not been consulted enough. Consent granted afterwards is not consent.","echoes":"Tuvalu","reference":"Module 2, Conclusions; Module 5, Theme 2","variants":[]},{"id":"1.3b","title":"Hold a national consultation with Keru and Tamal councils and both social partners, over ten weeks","note":"Slow. Builds legitimacy.","raw":{"choice":14,"voice":28,"work":7,"capacity":-12},"sets":["full_consult"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"You travel to Keru with the Minister and sit in the meeting house for two days. Elders ask for a clause on staying, for a say in the ballot rules and for the word 'climate' not to be used on visas. The Workers' Congress and the Federation each propose changes. The text improves and Halden's patience thins.","instrument":"Adequate, informed and ongoing consultation should take place with workers' and employers' organisations at all stages from policy design to implementation and evaluation, and dialogue with affected workers is essential (Module 4, Just Transition Guidelines applied to mobility).","practice":"Consultation takes time you may not have. It also produces the one asset the treaty cannot give you: a public that has already argued about it.","echoes":"Tuvalu","reference":"Module 4, Theme 1; Module 5, Theme 2","variants":[]},{"id":"1.3c","title":"Put it to a referendum","note":"Settles legitimacy. Halden may not wait.","raw":{"choice":10,"voice":30,"work":0,"capacity":-18},"sets":["referendum_held","halden_cooled"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The referendum takes six months. The text passes narrowly and the campaign is bruising. Halden does not withdraw its offer, but it treats Alvara as a partner that has to be asked twice. The finance and the ballot quota are both cut back.","instrument":"Public participation is a principle of just transition. Where social consensus is needed on the goal and the pathway, it is fundamental, not optional (Just Transition Guidelines, Module 2).","practice":"Critics of the Falepili treaty said it should have gone to referendum. A referendum settles who owns the decision. It does not settle whether the counterpart will honour the terms.","echoes":"Tuvalu","reference":"Module 2, Conclusions","variants":[]},{"id":"1.3d","title":"Consult the social partners through the tripartite committee and brief the councils afterwards","note":"Fast. Half the room.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":8,"work":14,"capacity":-2},"sets":["tripartite_only"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The tripartite committee meets twice in a week. The Federation asks for skills recognition and the Workers' Congress asks for an annex on fees and contracts. The councils on Keru and Tamal are briefed in the same week the text is signed. Nobody in Keru thinks that was consultation.","instrument":"Social dialogue is the mechanism by which social partners help formulate, implement and monitor policy (Module 5, Theme 2). It does not replace the participation of affected communities, which the course treats as a separate requirement.","practice":"Social partners get a seat, and communities get the radio. Employers will later know what the compact promises. Keru's elders will say they do not.","echoes":"Tuvalu","reference":"Module 5, Theme 2","variants":[]}]}]},{"num":2,"role":"The council representative","who":"Elected member and lands committee chair, Tamal Island Council","place":"Evacuation centre, Marau, Republic of Alvara","year":"Year 1. The weeks after Cyclone Edra.","brief":"Tamal has about 1,500 people, a lagoon, mangroves and land held in common by the community for nearly two centuries. A house is a lease from the Council, and any business needs a majority vote of residents. Cyclone Edra was the strongest storm on record in your sea, and all of Tamal was evacuated to Marau within three days. A week later the government announced a redevelopment plan and a foreign investor, Meridian, announced an interest.","lens":"You have a seat and a vote and very little else. The government has the army, the budget and the investor. Notice what you do have that cannot be taken away: the register of who holds what, the history, and the right to be asked.","bridge":"Whatever happened on Tamal, Keru's sea kept rising and the ballot places did not stretch. Two years later, 400 households have been moved to Vale, an upland district on the main island, in houses the government built. Some of them are from Tamal, and could not go home. Put on a different jacket: you are about to meet them.","dossier":{"case":"The Repeal of the Barbuda Land Act","tag":"Caribbean","points":["Hurricane Irma (2017, Category 5) left 95 per cent of buildings damaged. All 1,700 residents were evacuated to Antigua. A local official later insisted the island was damaged, not destroyed.","A week after the storm the government announced plans to open land to sale. In 2018 the communal land law was repealed in favour of individual deeds and a national land system, while residents were still displaced.","The airport was expanded before water and electricity were restored. The order barring return was lifted after three weeks. Two years on, people still lived in tents.","Residents went to court. A temporary injunction was granted in 2018 and in 2022 the court ruled they had no standing. The UN human rights office expressed concern. Even supporters of investment complained they were not consulted."],"gq":"Barbuda guiding questions 1 to 5 (a human rights based approach, unintended outcomes of the Land Act, the voice of ILO constituents, lessons for other SIDS, safeguards shared through South South cooperation)."},"scenes":[{"id":"2.1","title":"The return","situation":"Three weeks after Edra, the state of emergency order still bars return. Construction crews are on Tamal building an airstrip extension for private jets. Your constituents are in school halls in Marau. The Ministry says the island is destroyed and uninhabitable. A UN damage assessment you have seen says 95 per cent damaged. Water and power are down and the clinic roof is gone.","options":[{"id":"2.1a","title":"Accept the order and wait for the Ministry's schedule","note":"No confrontation. No leverage.","raw":{"choice":-16,"voice":-10,"work":-4,"capacity":4},"sets":["waited"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The Ministry thanks the Council for its patience. Weeks become months. The airstrip is completed first. By the time the first families are allowed home, many have taken work in Marau and the school hall has become a community that does not want to go back.","instrument":"The Guiding Principles on Internal Displacement protect rights before, during and after displacement, including the freedom to seek employment (Principle 22). Waiting is not a choice the Principles ask displaced people to make for the state.","practice":"In Barbuda, residents were blocked from returning for three weeks and construction crews were allowed in before they were. Two years later, many still lived in tents.","echoes":"Barbuda","reference":"Module 2, Theme 2","variants":[]},{"id":"2.1b","title":"Organise your own damage survey with fishers and engineers from the Workers' Congress","note":"Needs volunteers and a boat.","raw":{"choice":10,"voice":12,"work":4,"capacity":-4},"sets":["own_damage_data"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"Eleven volunteers sail over for four days. Their survey counts the homes, the boats and the wells that can be repaired. About seven in ten homes are damaged, not destroyed. The Ministry disputes the figures and cannot ignore them. The Workers' Congress asks to publish them jointly.","instrument":"The ILO and FAO Livelihood Assessment Toolkit is built around a baseline, a first impact appraisal and a detailed assessment, and it is meant to include migrant workers and displaced populations (Module 4, Theme 1).","practice":"The difference between damaged and destroyed is the difference between repair and a clean slate. In the Barbuda brief, the chair of the island council's fisheries division said exactly that.","echoes":"Barbuda","reference":"Module 4, Theme 1","variants":[]},{"id":"2.1c","title":"Go to court for an injunction and the right to return","note":"A principled route. Standing is uncertain.","raw":{"choice":4,"voice":6,"work":-2,"capacity":-4},"sets":["litigated"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"A judge grants a temporary injunction on the airstrip within a fortnight. The government challenges your right to bring the case. The case outlasts the Council's term, and the injunction is overturned on appeal.","instrument":"The Guiding Principles on Internal Displacement set out rights before, during and after displacement. They are not binding on their own, and much work is still needed to bring them into national law (Module 2).","practice":"In Barbuda, residents obtained a temporary injunction in 2018. In 2022 the court ruled that they had no standing. Litigation protects a principle and costs years.","echoes":"Barbuda","reference":"Module 2, Theme 2","variants":[]},{"id":"2.1d","title":"Trade: accept the airstrip in return for water, power and the clinic first, and a dated return","note":"Pragmatic. Concedes the airstrip.","raw":{"choice":14,"voice":-2,"work":5,"capacity":2},"sets":["phased_return"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The Ministry agrees to restore water, power and the clinic roof within ninety days and to lift the order in two phases. The airstrip goes ahead. Returning families are first on the payroll for the repair work. The Council is criticised for letting the airstrip through, and the first families are home in four months.","instrument":"Recommendation No. 205 puts decent employment generation at the centre of recovery after disasters, and asks for local economic recovery with a special focus on livelihoods (Module 4, Theme 1).","practice":"In Barbuda the airport came before the water and the power. A dated trade is better than none and worse than first things first, but sometimes it is what the room allows.","echoes":"Barbuda","reference":"Module 4, Theme 1","variants":[]}]},{"id":"2.2","title":"The land bill","situation":"The government tables a bill to replace the Tamal land law with the national registered land system. Every leaseholder would receive an individual deed that can be sold or mortgaged. The Prime Minister says it will let families borrow to rebuild. Meridian, which has offered to build a resort and marina on the lagoon shore, says it cannot invest without secure title. Under the existing law, land cannot be sold and a project needs a majority vote of residents. Most residents are still in Marau.","options":[{"id":"2.2a","title":"Accept individual deeds, with a community right of first refusal and a ten year bar on sales to non residents","note":"A compromise you can defend.","raw":{"choice":4,"voice":2,"work":4,"capacity":4},"sets":["deeds_safeguards"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The government accepts most of your amendments, and drops the bar on sales to non residents to five years. The Council keeps a right of first refusal. Banks accept the deeds as collateral. In the first year, a third of families borrow to rebuild.","instrument":"Rights based relocation and rebuilding depends on transparency, accountability and legal safeguards, as in Fiji's Planned Relocation Guidelines (Module 2, Box 3).","practice":"Safeguards on paper hold only if a body can enforce them. Ask who that is, and whether it has its own budget.","echoes":"Barbuda","reference":"Module 2, Box 3","variants":[{"flag":"cabinet_signed","text":"Since the Halden compact was signed by cabinet alone, the Council reads the amendment process as theatre, and the government agrees to fewer of your changes. The five year bar becomes two.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":-6,"work":0,"capacity":0}}]},{"id":"2.2b","title":"Reject the bill and insist that residents' consent, by the existing majority vote, is the only lawful route, including for evacuees in Marau","note":"The strongest claim. Needs everyone present.","raw":{"choice":14,"voice":28,"work":-2,"capacity":-6},"sets":["consent_rule"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"You arrange a vote in the school halls. Turnout is 83 per cent and the result is clear. The government says the vote has no legal force and that the emergency order suspends the old law. The Council keeps the register and the minutes, and refuses to lease land to anyone without the vote.","instrument":"The Just Transition Guidelines say affected populations must be engaged in decision making, and the course stresses that populations affected by climate change are often marginalised from the policies that govern their mobility (Module 2, Rights).","practice":"In Barbuda a land system nearly two centuries old, with a consent rule built in, was replaced in months while residents were scattered. Those who supported investment also said they were not consulted.","echoes":"Barbuda","reference":"Module 2, Theme 1 (Rights)","variants":[{"flag":"own_damage_data","text":"Your survey weakens the premise of the emergency order. A radio debate quotes your figures, and two government members of the Council abstain rather than back the bill.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":6,"work":0,"capacity":2}},{"flag":"full_consult","text":"The consultation forum from the Halden compact is still in place, and Tamal's councillors sit on it. You use it to table the demand, and it carries more weight than it would have otherwise.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":4,"work":0,"capacity":0}}]},{"id":"2.2c","title":"Take the deeds and advise families to sell early while prices are high","note":"Cash now. Land gone.","raw":{"choice":-16,"voice":-6,"work":5,"capacity":2},"sets":["deeds_sold"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"Meridian's agents open an office in the evacuation centre. Within a year, about a third of the lagoon frontage has been sold at prices that look high to families who have lost everything. Some of the cash goes into rebuilding and some goes to debts. The land is not coming back.","instrument":"Land is bound to livelihood as well as to identity. The Tuvalu brief points out that for many Pacific communities, losing access to ancestral land creates landless workers and new dependencies, a major concern for the ILO (Module 2).","practice":"Cash is a one off and landlessness is not. Families who sell on a bad day are rarely the families who profit from the next ten years of tourism.","echoes":"Tuvalu","reference":"Module 2, Theme 1","variants":[]},{"id":"2.2d","title":"Appeal outside: the UN human rights system, the ILO's constituents, regional bodies and the press","note":"Slow. Reputation is the lever.","raw":{"choice":2,"voice":8,"work":2,"capacity":-2},"sets":["intl_appeal"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"A letter from you, the Workers' Congress and a regional network reaches the UN human rights system and the ILO's constituents. The government denies the allegations. A foreign journalist visits Marau and writes about the evacuees. Meridian's lead investor asks for a briefing.","instrument":"The Paris Agreement says that states, when acting on climate change, should respect, promote and consider their human rights obligations (Module 2, Rights). Social partners also have a role in advocacy at the international level (Module 3, Theme 5).","practice":"In Barbuda, the UN human rights office eventually raised concerns. International attention does not stop a bulldozer. It changes what an investor can say to its own board.","echoes":"Barbuda","reference":"Module 2, Theme 2; Module 3, Theme 5","variants":[{"flag":"litigated","text":"The standing ruling from your own case is quoted against you in the government's reply. It also shows foreign observers how little domestic remedy was left.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":2,"work":0,"capacity":0}}]}]},{"id":"2.3","title":"The investor's offer","situation":"Meridian publishes its offer: 600 construction jobs, a community fund of one per cent of sales, a local hiring target of half the workforce and a pledge to respect Tamal's heritage. It says nothing about collective agreements, safety standards or who will be the employer of record. Evacuated fishers and farmers need income now. The Workers' Congress has asked whether the Council wants it at the table. The Employers' Federation has not been called. Meridian says the offer lapses in thirty days.","options":[{"id":"2.3a","title":"Accept the offer as published and get people to work","note":"Income now. Terms later.","raw":{"choice":-6,"voice":-4,"work":5,"capacity":6},"sets":["jobs_no_agreement"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"Construction starts within six weeks. Two subcontractors hire most of the workers, some paid in cash, with no written contract and no safety equipment. A scaffold fails in the third month. The community fund is paid, but the fund's trustees are chosen by Meridian.","instrument":"Recommendation No. 205 asks governments to create incentives for multinational enterprises to cooperate with national enterprises, to promote decent work and to undertake human rights due diligence, taking the Tripartite Declaration on multinational enterprises into account (Module 4, Theme 1).","practice":"Local hiring targets mean nothing without enforceable terms. Jobs on the shore are often the first formal jobs a community sees and the first place its workers learn they can be replaced.","echoes":"Barbuda","reference":"Module 4, Theme 1","variants":[]},{"id":"2.3b","title":"Counter with a local benefit agreement: union recognition, safety standards, local hiring, training and a Council seat","note":"Slow. Needs a tripartite table.","raw":{"choice":6,"voice":8,"work":22,"capacity":-2},"sets":["local_benefit_agreement"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The Workers' Congress and, for the first time, the Employers' Federation sit down with Meridian and the Council. The Federation's lawyers draft the agreement. It takes ten weeks and Meridian loses its lead investor's patience. The agreement is signed with the minimum local hiring figure cut from half to forty per cent, and the safety standards intact.","instrument":"Social dialogue and collective agreements can ensure decent wages and working conditions and recognise the rights of climate migrants in the workplace. Employers' organisations can promote compliance with labour standards among members (Module 5, Theme 2).","practice":"Local content only means something if it is written down, and written down means someone has to be at the table: the union and the Employers' Federation, not just the Council. It is the first time a tripartite table has worked on Tamal.","echoes":"Barbuda","reference":"Module 5, Theme 2","variants":[{"flag":"intl_appeal","text":"Because the appeal raised Meridian's reputational risk, the company's lawyers concede the safety standards in the first meeting.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":2,"work":4,"capacity":0}}]},{"id":"2.3c","title":"Decline Meridian. Rebuild with a community enterprise: a cash for work crew restoring mangroves and housing, and a fishers' cooperative","note":"Local. Needs a financier.","raw":{"choice":18,"voice":10,"work":5,"capacity":-7},"sets":["community_enterprise"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"A cash for work crew of 120 people restores the mangrove edge, clears the lagoon channel and rebuilds the clinic. A fishers' cooperative starts selling into Marau. Income is lower than Meridian's wages. The money comes from a regional donor and runs out in fourteen months.","instrument":"Green works are employment intensive programmes that restore public infrastructure, community assets and natural areas for adaptation and disaster response. In the ILO's Fiji pilot, cash for work crews with safety training built four houses in fifteen days (Module 4, Box 4).","practice":"Cash for work pays quickly and builds the assets a community lives on. It needs a financier, and the financier you have is the one you have just turned down.","echoes":"Handbook","reference":"Module 4, Theme 2 and Box 4","variants":[{"flag":"phased_return","text":"Returning families, already on the repair payroll from the phased return, form the first crews. The crews are on site in two weeks rather than six.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":0,"work":4,"capacity":2}}]},{"id":"2.3d","title":"Ask for a moratorium on sales, citizenship by investment links and construction until a land use plan is adopted","note":"Buys time. Loses the investor.","raw":{"choice":10,"voice":10,"work":-4,"capacity":-5},"sets":["moratorium"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The Prime Minister calls the moratorium unrealistic and the Council calls it the only responsible position. Meridian withdraws its lead investor and keeps the option open. A land use plan is drafted over eight months with the Council, the Federation and the Workers' Congress. Nothing is built in the meantime.","instrument":"National adaptation planning should integrate human mobility and be developed with a range of stakeholders, and should be mainstreamed into wider development strategies (Module 2, risk reduction).","practice":"A moratorium buys time and loses the investor. In Barbuda, new investment projects were linked to citizenship by investment, which changes who counts as a resident.","echoes":"Barbuda","reference":"Module 2, Theme 1","variants":[{"flag":"deeds_sold","text":"A third of the frontage has already been sold, so the moratorium covers only what is left. Buyers sue for breach of contract.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":0,"work":0,"capacity":-2}}]}]}]},{"num":3,"role":"The union organiser","who":"Organiser, Agricultural, Fishing and Construction Workers' Union (Workers' Congress affiliate)","place":"Vale district, main island, Republic of Alvara","year":"Year 3. Two years after the first families arrived.","brief":"Vale is an upland district forty minutes from Marau. The government has built 400 houses there for families from Keru and from Tamal. Each house has a yard. The land for farming was never allocated. No ministry has responsibility for relocated people. Most men and women who fished or farmed now do day labour on building sites in Marau, or domestic work in the capital. Your union has 40 members among about 1,100 working age residents, and all of them are in formal construction.","lens":"Your members are the ones with a contract. Everyone else at Vale is who you have to organise, if you are going to organise anyone. Notice how much of what you can do depends on what happened in the first two acts.","bridge":"Four years after the Halden compact, the government convenes the first full Tripartite Climate Mobility Council to adopt a national compact. The Workers' Congress has a seat. The Employers' Federation has the chair, and it has not yet said what it will put on the table. You are about to find out, from the other side of it.","dossier":{"case":"Social Protection and Climate Resilience in Zimbabwe","tag":"Southern Africa","points":["About 139,000 people were displaced by climate related disasters between 2009 and 2023. Planned relocation is a growing response, but it rarely comes with a policy on livelihoods.","In Tsholotsho the relocation did not allocate enough land for farming. Relocated daily wage workers were not members of cooperatives or the agricultural workers' union, and employers took advantage of their situation.","Young women end up in low paid domestic work, young men in construction, and many try to cross the border informally. No ministry has responsibility for displaced persons.","Social protection can build assets and resilience, but the case asks how people can graduate from support, who participates in design, and how grievances are heard."],"gq":"Zimbabwe guiding questions on identifying needs, engagement and participation, collaboration with local organisations, resilience building and policy planning."},"scenes":[{"id":"3.1","title":"The first month","situation":"You have two organisers and six weeks before another 150 families arrive. Day labourers gather at the junction at dawn, and contractors pick by the day at rates that vary by the hour. The district office has not said what land, if any, will be allocated. Three things are urgent, and you can do one properly.","options":[{"id":"3.1a","title":"Sign up the day labourers at the junction, women in domestic work included, as an informal workers' chapter","note":"Reaches the many. Costs organising time.","raw":{"choice":2,"voice":8,"work":7,"capacity":-4},"sets":["informal_chapter"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"Within a month you have 210 members, two thirds of them women. Dues are tiny, the first meeting is in the school yard, and the first demand is for a written daily rate. Your two organisers are exhausted. Contractors who used to pick at the junction now ask who the union leader is.","instrument":"Freedom of association and the effective recognition of the right to collective bargaining is one of the five fundamental principles and rights at work. The Transition from the Informal to the Formal Economy Recommendation, 2015 (No. 204), applies to workers and economic units in the informal economy (Module 4; Module 5).","practice":"In Tsholotsho, relocated daily wage workers were not members of the agricultural workers' union or of cooperatives, and employers took advantage of their situation. This is the same gap, closed early.","echoes":"Zimbabwe","reference":"Module 4, Theme 2; Module 5, Theme 1","variants":[]},{"id":"3.1b","title":"Negotiate with the district office on land and livelihoods before any more families arrive","note":"Policy first. Fewer people reached.","raw":{"choice":16,"voice":6,"work":5,"capacity":-1},"sets":["land_first"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The district officer takes four meetings to say yes. Half a hectare per household is allocated, subject to survey. A livelihoods desk opens in the district office. The 150 arriving families come to a better settlement than the first 400 did. You have not signed up a single new member.","instrument":"Good practice in planned relocation gives high prominence to livelihoods, including land for agricultural livelihoods and access to finance and skills, in advance of the relocation (Module 4, Box 10).","practice":"In Tsholotsho, the relocation did not allocate enough land for farming. Asking before the next families arrive costs less than asking after.","echoes":"Zimbabwe","reference":"Module 4, Theme 2 and Box 10","variants":[{"flag":"stay_package","text":"The ring fenced fund from the Halden talks has a livelihoods window for relocated households. The district officer can point to money, and the allocation is confirmed in two meetings, not four.","raw":{"choice":2,"voice":0,"work":4,"capacity":2}}]},{"id":"3.1c","title":"Start a savings and credit group and a purchasing cooperative that anyone can join","note":"Builds solidarity without the state's permission.","raw":{"choice":6,"voice":4,"work":5,"capacity":-1},"sets":["coop_started"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"Sixty households join in the first fortnight. The group pools its transfers, lends small amounts and buys seed and tools in bulk. It does not change a day labourer's rate but it does change what she can survive. Some members are not union members, and say so.","instrument":"Cooperatives and the social and solidarity economy can create jobs for displaced and receiving communities, and a savings group is a standard tool of social protection co production (Module 4; Module 5).","practice":"Cooperatives hold people together without needing permission from anyone. They do not, on their own, change a daily wage.","echoes":"Zimbabwe","reference":"Module 4, Theme 2","variants":[{"flag":"community_enterprise","text":"Two organisers from Tamal's fishers' cooperative come to Vale. They bring a model, a rule book and credit with the first lender, and the group is running in three weeks.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":2,"work":4,"capacity":0}}]},{"id":"3.1d","title":"Focus on your existing members in formal construction, where you have bargaining power","note":"Safe. Leaves the junction alone.","raw":{"choice":-4,"voice":-2,"work":4,"capacity":4},"sets":["insiders_only"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"You win a better safety agreement and a modest wage increase for the 40. The junction stays as it was. The next batch of families arrives, and many of them are hired there on the day, at the day's rate.","instrument":"International labour standards and the fundamental principles and rights at work apply to all workers, including those who have been relocated within a country, without exception (Module 2, Theme 2).","practice":"Insiders' protection is real and it is a ceiling. Employers will ask who you actually represent, and the honest answer is: not the people at the junction.","echoes":"Zimbabwe","reference":"Module 2, Theme 2","variants":[]}]},{"id":"3.2","title":"The support scheme","situation":"The Ministry of Social Development launches the Vale Resilience Support: a monthly cash transfer to relocated households for 24 months, plus an optional public works programme. Eligibility lists were drawn from the relocation register, not from a needs assessment. Host community families in the lower village, who are poorer than many relocated households, are not on the list. The Ministry invites the union to comment before the cabinet paper is finalised.","options":[{"id":"3.2a","title":"Support the cash transfer as designed. Speed matters","note":"Hunger first. Design later.","raw":{"choice":-8,"voice":-2,"work":5,"capacity":6},"sets":["targeted_cash"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The first payments reach households in five weeks, directly into accounts. Food insecurity falls. Hostility from the lower village rises, and a handful of families are wrongly left off the list and have no one to appeal to. In month 23, the Ministry asks what happens next.","instrument":"The Social Protection Floors Recommendation (No. 202) sets out adequacy, comprehensiveness, sustainability and universality as principles. The course notes that targeted measures dominate climate related social protection and that direct payments reduce leakages (Module 5).","practice":"A targeted transfer with a fixed end date is a bridge to nowhere unless something is built on the other side. The Zimbabwe brief asks exactly how people can graduate from support.","echoes":"Zimbabwe","reference":"Module 5, Theme 2","variants":[{"flag":"land_first","text":"Because land has already been allocated, the transfer works as bridging income while the first harvest comes in. Households can see a route off support within eighteen months.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":0,"work":5,"capacity":0}},{"flag":"coop_started","text":"The savings group means households save and lend part of their transfers locally, and money circulates in Vale instead of leaving it on Friday.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":0,"work":4,"capacity":0}}]},{"id":"3.2b","title":"Push the public works option: guaranteed days of work, a wage floor, safety training and union supervised crews","note":"Builds assets and skills. Costs more.","raw":{"choice":8,"voice":4,"work":22,"capacity":-6},"sets":["green_works_ega"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The Ministry agrees to pilot 80 guaranteed days a year, paid at a floor rate, with extra days after a cyclone. Crews build terraces, drainage and a school road. The union trains safety stewards. The budget for the first year is three times the cash only option and the Treasury asks if it is sustainable.","instrument":"India's rural employment guarantee provides at least 100 days of wage work a year, with 50 extra days after droughts, floods and cyclones. The course treats such programmes as a way of building the assets that underpin livelihoods (Module 5, Theme 2; Module 4, Box 4).","practice":"Public works builds assets and skills and gives people something to graduate from. It is administratively heavy, and it needs a union at the gate to keep wages honest.","echoes":"Handbook","reference":"Module 4, Box 4; Module 5, Theme 2","variants":[{"flag":"land_first","text":"Public works crews build the access road and irrigation to the new plots, so that land and work reinforce one another.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":0,"work":4,"capacity":2}},{"flag":"informal_chapter","text":"Your chapter supplies the crew leaders and the safety stewards. Pay disputes are settled at the gate, and not in the press.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":4,"work":2,"capacity":0}}]},{"id":"3.2c","title":"Push inclusive coverage: include host community households in the lower village at a lower benefit","note":"Calms resentment. Spreads the budget.","raw":{"choice":4,"voice":6,"work":5,"capacity":-6},"sets":["inclusive_coverage"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The Ministry accepts coverage of the lower village at 60 per cent of the benefit. Resentment falls and the district's mayor stops blaming relocated families for the water shortages. The scheme costs 40 per cent more than planned and the Treasury asks that something be cut.","instrument":"Recommendation No. 205 says interventions should also benefit host community members, to improve their access to decent work and foster social cohesion (Module 4, Theme 1).","practice":"Including host households calms the resentment that otherwise makes relocated families the target. It also spreads the budget thinner, which is the argument the Treasury will use.","echoes":"Zimbabwe","reference":"Module 4, Theme 1","variants":[]},{"id":"3.2d","title":"Accept the scheme as designed, but ask for a grievance and redress mechanism and participatory monitoring","note":"A route to be heard.","raw":{"choice":4,"voice":12,"work":2,"capacity":-2},"sets":["grievance_mech"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The Ministry agrees to a grievance desk in the district office, a committee of residents to monitor payments and a quarterly public report. In the first three months 90 complaints are logged, a third of them about wrongful exclusion. Most are resolved in under a month. The committee is chaired by a woman from Keru.","instrument":"The UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights set effectiveness criteria for operational level grievance mechanisms, including accessibility, transparency and a route that does not run through the person being complained about.","practice":"A grievance route that does not run back through whoever distributes the money is rare. It is also the single feature that makes the rest of the scheme believable to people who have already been told what to do once too often.","echoes":"Zimbabwe","reference":"Module 5, Theme 2","variants":[]}]},{"id":"3.3","title":"Below the floor","situation":"A building contractor and a farm operator on the edge of Marau are hiring Vale residents at about 60 per cent of the sector wage floor, with no written contracts and no protective equipment. Two young women who work as live in domestic workers in the capital have come to you. One has not been paid for three months. The other cannot leave because her employer is holding her identity card. The Employers' Federation's secretary says he has heard some rumours.","options":[{"id":"3.3a","title":"File complaints with the labour inspectorate for each case","note":"Formal route. Slow and exposing.","raw":{"choice":4,"voice":2,"work":5,"capacity":-2},"sets":["inspection_complaint"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The inspectorate has six inspectors for the whole island. It opens two of your four cases, visits the building site after three weeks and issues a notice. The farm closes its gate. The domestic worker whose card is held is helped out by a neighbour, not by the inspectorate.","instrument":"The elimination of forced or compulsory labour is a fundamental principle and right at work, and retention of identity documents by an employer is a recognised indicator of it. Labour inspection is the state's tool for enforcing it.","practice":"Complaints create a record, and a record is what a later case needs. They also expose workers who have no savings to losing the job before the inspector arrives.","echoes":"Zimbabwe","reference":"Module 5, Theme 1 and Box 14","variants":[{"flag":"grievance_mech","text":"The grievance desk at Vale takes the statements, fixes the evidence and sends the case to the inspector within a week. The workers do not need to go to Marau.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":2,"work":4,"capacity":2}},{"flag":"informal_chapter","text":"Your chapter's members come forward as witnesses, and the case is not left with two frightened women.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":2,"work":2,"capacity":0}}]},{"id":"3.3b","title":"Offer the Employers' Federation a deal: a registry of trained Vale workers in return for a wage floor and written contracts","note":"A seat for the other side.","raw":{"choice":2,"voice":4,"work":14,"capacity":-1},"sets":["employer_deal"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The Federation's secretary has been looking for a way in. A sector agreement is signed in eight weeks. Member firms commit to written contracts, the wage floor and safety equipment for registry workers. The two firms that employed the workers at 60 per cent are not members.","instrument":"Collective agreements can ensure decent wages and working conditions and give practical recognition to the rights of climate migrants. Employers' organisations can support fair recruitment and access to skills development for workers from climate affected areas (Module 5, Theme 1 and 2).","practice":"A sector deal covers hundreds of workers in a stroke, but only inside the firms that belong to the Federation. Ask who is not at the table.","echoes":"Zimbabwe","reference":"Module 5, Theme 2","variants":[{"flag":"informal_chapter","text":"Because you represent 210 workers, and not 40, the Federation treats you as a counterpart and not a petitioner. The registry is built on your membership list.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":4,"work":4,"capacity":0}},{"flag":"insiders_only","text":"The Federation's secretary asks who exactly you speak for, and the honest answer is the 40. The deal is a small one.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":-4,"work":-6,"capacity":0}}]},{"id":"3.3c","title":"Call a stoppage at the building site and march on the Ministry","note":"Visible. Risky for those with no savings.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":8,"work":2,"capacity":-6},"sets":["action_taken"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The stoppage lasts three days. The march is covered by the evening news. The contractor's response is to hire a new crew from the junction. The Minister agrees to a meeting, and a working group is formed. Eleven workers who joined the stoppage are not rehired.","instrument":"Freedom of association and the effective recognition of the right to collective bargaining protect collective action. Collective bargaining systems tend to be weaker in countries where climate impacts on the working environment are strongest (Module 5, Theme 2).","practice":"Collective action puts an issue on the front page and risks the jobs of people who have no savings. Workers outside the union are exposed first.","echoes":"Zimbabwe","reference":"Module 5, Theme 2","variants":[{"flag":"informal_chapter","text":"With 210 members the stoppage holds beyond the first day, and the contractor cannot replace the whole crew from the junction.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":4,"work":4,"capacity":0}},{"flag":"insiders_only","text":"With only 40 members the stoppage is easy to replace from the junction. The contractor does not even call the Minister.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":-4,"work":-4,"capacity":0}}]},{"id":"3.3d","title":"Open a worker run hiring hall at Vale with a skills programme, and refer workers only to employers who sign a code","note":"Replaces the junction. Needs running.","raw":{"choice":8,"voice":4,"work":11,"capacity":-4},"sets":["hiring_hall"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The hall opens in a former store with a roster, a noticeboard and a basic safety course. It places 140 workers in four months. Two employers sign the code and three refuse. The hall charges no fees, and workers say it is the first recruiter they trust.","instrument":"The General Principles and Operational Guidelines for Fair Recruitment state that no recruitment fees or related costs should be charged to workers. The course also highlights public employment services and TVET as central to employability (Module 4, Box 8; Module 5, Theme 1).","practice":"A hiring hall is a recruiter you control. Run well, it gives people an alternative to the junction and to brokers. Run badly, it becomes one more intermediary.","echoes":"Zimbabwe","reference":"Module 4, Theme 2 and Box 8","variants":[]}]}]},{"num":4,"role":"The employers' representative","who":"President, Alvara Employers' Federation","place":"Tripartite Climate Mobility Council, Marau","year":"Year 4. Four years after the Halden compact.","brief":"Your members run construction firms, the hotels, a fish processing plant, the banks and the largest farm. The Federation was not at the table when the Halden compact was drafted, was not called when Tamal was being rebuilt, and was asked about Vale only after the union came knocking. Now the Prime Minister has called the first full tripartite council to adopt a national Climate Mobility Compact, and the government has asked three things of employers: jobs, skills and money.","lens":"Employers are neither the villain nor the hero of this story. The question is whether your members are ready to sit at the table, and what you will put on it. Everything that happened in the previous three acts decides what is on offer.","bridge":"","dossier":{"case":"All three dossiers, and Modules 4 and 5 of the course","tag":"Synthesis","points":["An ILO evaluation found that employers' and workers' organisations are engaging with climate impacts on their members, but that this has not extended to systematic social dialogue and tripartism in policy processes.","Employers' organisations can promote compliance with international labour standards, support fair recruitment and equal access to skills development. They also lead the transition of production practices and TVET.","Financing is the Achilles heel of social protection. Options include contributory revenue, taxes, reallocating spending and aid, but social protection should not exclude or surcharge climate mobile workers.","ILO work on South South and SIDS to SIDS cooperation (2018 to 2023) included an exchange on trade union engagement between Asia Pacific and Caribbean SIDS."],"gq":"The South South and Triangular Cooperation guiding questions in the Tuvalu and Barbuda briefs, and the 'collaboration with local organisations' and 'policy planning' questions in the Zimbabwe brief."},"scenes":[{"id":"4.1","title":"Jobs and skills","situation":"Your members have vacancies in construction, care, tourism and food processing. About 2,100 relocated workers and Tamal returnees want work. At the same time, young Alvaran nurses and tradespeople are leaving for Halden, where their qualifications are partly unrecognised. The Minister asks the Federation for a commitment before the council meets.","options":[{"id":"4.1a","title":"Pledge priority hiring of relocated workers, with a training subsidy and written contracts","note":"Costs members now. Builds the workforce.","raw":{"choice":8,"voice":4,"work":22,"capacity":-6},"sets":["hiring_pledge"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"Members commit to fill one in four new vacancies from the relocated workforce, with written contracts and a state funded training subsidy for six months. The Federation's lawyers draft a standard contract. Three members object, and one resigns.","instrument":"Employers' organisations can support fair recruitment and equal access to skills development and social protection for workers from climate affected areas (Module 5, Theme 1). Recommendation No. 205 calls for local economic recovery and decent employment generation (Module 4).","practice":"A hiring pledge is cheap to announce and expensive to keep. It works when the workforce is ready and the contract is standard. In its absence it becomes an advert.","echoes":"Zimbabwe","reference":"Module 4, Theme 1; Module 5, Theme 1","variants":[{"flag":"green_works_ega","text":"Workers come off the public works crews with certified safety and trade skills, and members take them on within a month.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":0,"work":4,"capacity":2}},{"flag":"hiring_hall","text":"The hiring hall's roster is ready and its code is already signed by two employers. Placement takes weeks instead of months.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":2,"work":4,"capacity":2}},{"flag":"employer_deal","text":"The Federation is already a party to the sector agreement on wage floors and written contracts, so the pledge costs less than members feared.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":0,"work":2,"capacity":4}}]},{"id":"4.1b","title":"Propose a skills recognition and TVET partnership between employers, TVET institutions, the unions and Halden's authorities","note":"Slower. Works at both ends.","raw":{"choice":16,"voice":4,"work":14,"capacity":-6},"sets":["skills_partnership"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"A working group drafts a recognition framework for care and trades, a bridging course for local TVET colleges and a roadmap for validating prior learning. It takes a year to show results. In the meantime, members keep losing staff.","instrument":"The ILO Human Resources Development Recommendation, 2004 (No. 195), and Convention No. 143 call for recognition of qualifications after consultation with social partners. The ILO's question set for bilateral labour agreements covers recognition of prior learning, pre departure training and the role of employers in skills governance (Module 4, Box 8).","practice":"Skills recognition is where a mobility agreement turns into a good job. Qualified people can use their skills at both ends of the journey.","echoes":"Tuvalu","reference":"Module 4, Theme 2 and Box 8","variants":[{"flag":"labour_annex","text":"The labour annex signed in act one already holds a recognition schedule for care and trades. You have a counterpart in Halden, and the first cohort is certified within six months.","raw":{"choice":2,"voice":0,"work":5,"capacity":4}},{"flag":"tied_permits","text":"Workers who left on the seasonal scheme cannot use the new recognition, because their permits tie them to one employer. The partnership reaches the ballot migrants only.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":0,"work":-2,"capacity":0}}]},{"id":"4.1c","title":"Ask for flexible contracts and a lower entry wage for relocated workers while they are trained","note":"Cheap in year one. Costly after.","raw":{"choice":-10,"voice":-6,"work":-16,"capacity":16},"sets":["flex_contracts"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The government agrees to a two tier training wage for relocated workers, at 70 per cent of the floor for twelve months. Labour costs fall, vacancies fill and the Workers' Congress walks out of the working group. Within a year, the same firms report high turnover in the trainee group.","instrument":"The fundamental principles and rights at work include the elimination of discrimination in respect of employment and occupation. The course stresses that climate mobile workers should not be penalised by exclusion or surcharging (Module 5).","practice":"A two tier wage invites exploitation of the group you are trying to include, and resentment among local workers. It is a pattern repeated in many temporary labour schemes.","echoes":"Handbook","reference":"Module 2, Theme 2; Module 5, Theme 2","variants":[{"flag":"tied_permits","text":"Members cite Halden's own seasonal scheme as precedent for employer tied contracts, and the Workers' Congress cites the same scheme as the warning.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":-2,"work":-2,"capacity":0}},{"flag":"employer_deal","text":"The Federation is a party to the sector agreement, and the proposal contradicts it. Members who signed the agreement accuse the Federation of bad faith.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":-4,"work":-4,"capacity":-1}}]},{"id":"4.1d","title":"Say hiring is not the Federation's business and leave it to the government","note":"No cost. No seat.","raw":{"choice":-8,"voice":-8,"work":-12,"capacity":8},"sets":["employers_absent"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The Federation stays out of the working groups. The government writes the hiring rules and the Workers' Congress writes the amendments. Members find out about the rules from the gazette.","instrument":"Governments must involve social partners at all stages, from design to implementation and evaluation. The ILO has found that organisations' engagement with climate issues has not extended to systematic social dialogue in policy processes (Module 5, Theme 2).","practice":"Absence is a position too, and the one most likely to produce rules that employers cannot live with.","echoes":"Handbook","reference":"Module 5, Theme 2","variants":[{"flag":"tripartite_only","text":"You sat at the table for the Halden compact and know what it promised. Walking away from your own text is hard to defend.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":-4,"work":-2,"capacity":0}}]}]},{"id":"4.2","title":"Who pays","situation":"The draft Compact promises portable social protection for people who move or relocate, a stay in place fund for Keru and Tamal, and a joint monitoring body. It is costed at about three per cent of the national budget each year. The Treasury says there is no room. The Prime Minister asks how the private sector will contribute and where the remainder comes from.","options":[{"id":"4.2a","title":"Accept a shared payroll contribution: employers, workers and government each pay, with the formal sector carrying coverage for informal workers at first","note":"Predictable. Employers pay.","raw":{"choice":6,"voice":2,"work":18,"capacity":8},"sets":["payroll_share"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"A contribution of 1.5 per cent of payroll from employers and 1 per cent from workers is agreed, with the government topping up. The contribution funds portable coverage for relocated and returning workers. Three large members threaten to relocate their registered offices to a neighbour.","instrument":"The ILO lists increasing contributory revenue and the number of contributors among ways to finance social protection. Social protection financing should not privilege or penalise climate mobile workers (Module 5, Theme 2).","practice":"Contributory revenue is the most reliable source, and the one most dependent on the formal sector's size. Ask what share of workers it reaches.","echoes":"Zimbabwe","reference":"Module 5, Theme 2","variants":[{"flag":"inclusive_coverage","text":"Host community households are already covered, so the contribution base is wider and the rate can be lower.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":0,"work":2,"capacity":4}},{"flag":"targeted_cash","text":"The cash transfer is still running on donor money with no end date. The payroll share replaces that money and puts the scheme on a national footing.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":0,"work":2,"capacity":4}}]},{"id":"4.2b","title":"Take Halden's fund and let the government agree the terms","note":"Fast money. Whose strings?","raw":{"choice":-2,"voice":-8,"work":5,"capacity":22},"sets":["halden_funds"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The Treasury is relieved. Halden's money funds the stay in place fund and the first three years of portable coverage. The Council notes that the fund has no termination date and no consultation clause.","instrument":"Restitution and restorative justice ask highly industrialised countries to fund resilience and relocation for the countries least responsible for climate change. The Loss and Damage Fund and the Warsaw International Mechanism are part of that architecture (Module 2; Module 3).","practice":"The Falepili brief describes a transaction. Climate finance owed under the principle of common but differentiated responsibilities should not require foreign policy as collateral.","echoes":"Tuvalu","reference":"Module 2, Theme 1; Module 3, Theme 1","variants":[{"flag":"security_clause_open","text":"Halden pays quickly. The security chapter says the money is conditional on alignment on 'security matters'. The Compact does not say that anywhere, and everyone in the room knows it.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":-6,"work":0,"capacity":4}},{"flag":"clause_narrowed","text":"Halden's money comes through the narrowed text, with the review clause and a consultation duty. It arrives slower and cleaner.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":4,"work":0,"capacity":-1}},{"flag":"unbundled","text":"Halden says the finance chapter is still 'under discussion' and pays a small first tranche. The balance depends on the security talks.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":-2,"work":0,"capacity":-6}},{"flag":"halden_cooled","text":"Halden is lukewarm after the referendum, and the fund is a third of the size promised.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":0,"work":0,"capacity":-8}},{"flag":"regional_route","text":"No compact with Halden was ever signed, so there is no Halden fund. The regional facility offers technical help and not money, and you spend half the session finding that out.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":0,"work":0,"capacity":-14}}]},{"id":"4.2c","title":"Negotiate a community benefit levy with investors through the Tamal local benefit agreement","note":"Money from the people who profit.","raw":{"choice":6,"voice":6,"work":7,"capacity":8},"sets":["investor_levy"],"gate":"local_benefit_agreement","locked":"Not available. No agreement was signed with Meridian on Tamal that includes a local benefit clause, so there is nothing to attach a levy to.","what":"Meridian agrees to a levy of one per cent of project revenues, paid into the national stay in place fund and shared with the Tamal Council. The Employers' Federation signs as co guarantor. The levy raises less than the Treasury hoped, and more than anyone expected.","instrument":"Recommendation No. 205 asks states to create incentives for multinational enterprises to cooperate with national enterprises and to undertake human rights due diligence, taking the Tripartite Declaration on multinational enterprises into account (Module 4, Theme 1).","practice":"A levy is easier to collect when an agreement already exists and the parties know each other. Where there is none, there is nothing to attach it to.","echoes":"Barbuda","reference":"Module 4, Theme 1","variants":[{"flag":"deeds_sold","text":"A third of the lagoon frontage has already been sold, and the levy catches only the remainder.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":0,"work":0,"capacity":-2}},{"flag":"deeds_safeguards","text":"Because every sale passes through the community's right of first refusal, the levy is collected at the point of transfer.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":0,"work":0,"capacity":4}}]},{"id":"4.2d","title":"Go back to donors and the Loss and Damage Fund","note":"Slow. Dependent.","raw":{"choice":2,"voice":2,"work":4,"capacity":-2},"sets":["donor_dependent"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"A donor mission visits Vale, Keru and Tamal and writes a generous report. The first tranche of funding arrives in eighteen months and covers two of the three programmes. The Compact is written around the donor's calendar.","instrument":"The Loss and Damage Fund established at COP27 defines economic and non economic losses to include forced displacement and impacts on cultural heritage, human mobility and the lives and livelihoods of local communities (Module 3, Theme 1).","practice":"The course notes that social protection is often donor driven and not embedded in national institutions and political processes, and that this is the weakness of the model.","echoes":"Handbook","reference":"Module 3, Theme 1; Module 5, Theme 2","variants":[{"flag":"intl_appeal","text":"Because the appeal over Tamal made Alvara a known case, a loss and damage mission accepts your application and the first tranche arrives in half the time.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":2,"work":2,"capacity":12}}]}]},{"id":"4.3","title":"The Compact","situation":"The text on the table includes a stay in place fund, a land consent rule for community land, portable social protection, a tripartite monitoring body and a mandate to review partner agreements. Unions and employers have each marked a handful of clauses. The Prime Minister wants unanimity. The Federation's signature decides whether the Compact is tripartite.","options":[{"id":"4.3a","title":"Sign the Compact as drafted","note":"Full backing. Full cost.","raw":{"choice":10,"voice":8,"work":7,"capacity":-2},"sets":["compact_signed"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The Compact is adopted by acclamation. The Prime Minister thanks the Federation by name. The Treasury asks for a review of the costing in the first year. The Workers' Congress sends a bouquet.","instrument":"The Just Transition Guidelines call for strong social consensus on goals and pathways, and for social dialogue as an integral part of policy making at all levels (Module 2, Conclusions).","practice":"A full signature is easy to give and hard to withdraw. It is only worth as much as the monitoring body that holds the signatories to account.","echoes":"Handbook","reference":"Module 2, Conclusions; Module 5, Theme 2","variants":[{"flag":"security_clause_open","text":"Halden's ambassador sits in the public gallery. Nothing in the Compact touches Article 4, and the Workers' Congress notes it in the minutes.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":-6,"work":0,"capacity":0}},{"flag":"consent_rule","text":"The land consent rule in the Compact is the Tamal rule, which the Council defended in act two. The Compact is signed in Tamal's name as much as in the government's.","raw":{"choice":2,"voice":4,"work":0,"capacity":0}}]},{"id":"4.3b","title":"Sign with reservations on costs and on the land consent rule","note":"Backs the Compact. Keeps an exit.","raw":{"choice":2,"voice":2,"work":4,"capacity":4},"sets":["compact_reservations"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The Compact is adopted with a note of reservation recorded in the minutes. The Prime Minister is irritated. The Workers' Congress says reservations are the Federation's right. Investors read the reservation on the land consent rule as a sign of where employers' limits lie.","instrument":"The course stresses that governments should involve social partners from design to evaluation, and that social partners need strengthened capacities to engage and represent their constituencies (Module 5, Theme 2).","practice":"Reservations keep a seat at the table and keep the argument alive. They also tell every partner where the next dispute will be.","echoes":"Handbook","reference":"Module 5, Theme 2","variants":[{"flag":"consent_rule","text":"The reservation on the land consent rule is read in Tamal as a betrayal by the one partner the Council had come to trust.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":-6,"work":0,"capacity":0}},{"flag":"local_benefit_agreement","text":"The Federation signed the Tamal agreement and is known to honour it, so the reservation reads as a point about costing and not about principle.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":2,"work":0,"capacity":2}}]},{"id":"4.3c","title":"Withhold the Federation's signature until the financing is settled","note":"Leverage. Risk of a split council.","raw":{"choice":-8,"voice":-4,"work":-6,"capacity":2},"sets":["signature_withheld"],"gate":null,"locked":null,"what":"The council adjourns for two months. The Federation is praised by some members and criticised by others. The Prime Minister threatens to adopt the Compact without the Federation. The financing is settled in the end, on terms you did not set.","instrument":"Social dialogue is one of the four pillars of the ILO's decent work agenda, and strong social consensus is described as fundamental to a just transition (Module 5, Theme 2).","practice":"Withholding a signature is a lever, and levers have a cost. In every case in the course, those who stayed out of the room found the rules written without them.","echoes":"Handbook","reference":"Module 5, Theme 2","variants":[]},{"id":"4.3d","title":"Invoke the review clause in the Halden compact and bring Halden's labour and foreign ministries to the council","note":"Reopens the treaty. Only if you can.","raw":{"choice":16,"voice":12,"work":7,"capacity":-1},"sets":["review_invoked"],"gate":"clause_narrowed","locked":"Not available. The compact signed in act one contains no review clause, so there is no mechanism through which Alvara can reopen it.","what":"Halden's officials attend the second session. Article 4's list is re read, narrowed and published. The review is slow, and all three partners agree it is more honest than the first round. The Compact is signed with the review attached.","instrument":"The Just Transition Guidelines say international labour standards should be the basis on which governments design consultations leading to bilateral agreements, and that workers' and employers' organisations should be consulted at every stage (Module 4, Theme 1).","practice":"Very few small states can reopen a treaty with a larger neighbour. Where one can, it is because someone accepted a delay in the first act.","echoes":"Tuvalu","reference":"Module 4, Theme 1","variants":[{"flag":"referendum_held","text":"The referendum gave the review a democratic mandate, and Halden's officials cannot treat it as a disagreement between governments only.","raw":{"choice":0,"voice":4,"work":0,"capacity":0}}]}]}]}]};
